## Findings
1. advisory — brief:72 The "stable 96px offset" cannot be reached at either end of the queue. `scrollIntoView({block:"start"})` with `scroll-margin-block-start: 96px` is clamped by `.shell__main`'s scroll range. Moving with `k` or a click to the first item leaves `scrollTop` at 0, so that row stays at its natural position: about 86px at 1280, under the title and the 24px marker (shots/inbox-1280-focused.png). The last rows of the fourteen-record fixture cannot scroll up to 96px either. At 1280×900 the content below row 14 (its expansion, two disclosures and the Colophon) is far shorter than the 804px needed, so that row lands several hundred pixels lower. Served b3 measures only the seventh item (w407.spec.ts:176,193), so the brief promises more than the code delivers for the rows the proved fixture itself contains. The same applies to brief:25 and brief:92. Replacement for brief:72: append to its limit "the 96px offset holds where `.shell__main`'s scroll range allows; the first rows and rows near the queue end land at their clamped position, and only the seventh-of-fourteen and post-answer index-six cases are measured". [severity: S2] [class: spec×promise-unattainable-under-accepted-domain] — check: none: a served b3 row that presses `j` to the fourteenth item and `k` back to the first, asserting each lands at its clamped offset (or at 96 where reachable)
2. advisory — brief:25 Judged for sense, not conformance. Default 9 (the producer's call, not a Patron ruling) reveals on every `j`/`k`, even when the target row is already fully visible. With smooth motion, each keystroke slides the whole queue under a fixed cursor, and at 1280 the screen title scrolls away after one or two presses. That is defensible as a stable reading line, but it is a real trade-off against a reveal-only-when-needed policy. Neither the Patron nor a DIRECTION rule chose it, and the brief does not name it as an open question. Replacement for brief:25's second sentence: "The focused/expanded row lands at a stable 96px offset from that scroller's top (producer's call, Default 9, offered to the Patron against reveal-only-when-off-screen), below the screen-title position;" [severity: S4] [class: spec×prose-contradiction-under-revision] — check: none
3. advisory — brief:50 The DIRECTION §6 amendment in the prototype replaces "a 720px measure" with "One column filling the main pane … reading text … keeps a ~72ch measure". It does not record who ruled it or when. That leaves the next reader without the provenance this rule was missing in 5e5efd88, the reason it was wrong for a month. brief:19 and brief:50 also attribute the ruling differently: "producer made this design call under the Patron's request" versus "Patron-requested". Replacement for brief:50: "`docs/design/DIRECTION.md`: amend §6's Inbox width sentence to the full-main-pane queue with an inner ~72ch reading measure, citing the Patron's 2026-10-27 ruling that the agent-written 720px measure did not make sense." [severity: S4] [class: spec×prose-contradiction-under-revision] — check: none

## Verdict
The round-8 changes keep the input boundary sound. The server comparator in `Store.api.needsYou()` routes `openedAt` through schema's strict `instant()` and puts malformed or absent instants last, in stable source order. The view renders that order without sorting again, and the sticky marker is opaque, neutral ink. The full-width queue with its inner 72ch reading block makes sense on the 1280, 640 and 390 captures. The remaining items are a limit the reveal promise should state and two provenance/wording nits, all advisory for the build.
VERDICT: PASS
